  "summary": "Japanese stationery design characterized by cute, childlike aesthetics and playful motifs.",
  "description": "Features rounded shapes, pastel colors, and adorable character designs in a functional format. Emerged during the 1970s with brands like Sanrio and evolved into a major commercial aesthetic influencing global stationery design.",
  "culturalContext": "Kawaii stationery culture — Sanrio (1974 Hello Kitty) through mochi-pen cases, sticker flakes (kamu sei), washi tape (mt brand) and letter-set handwriting culture — industrialized cuteness as consumer graphic language: pastel palettes, rounded-corner everything, food-with-faces, and limited-run collaboration culture. Japanese stationery stores (Itoya, Loft, Tokyu Hands) built tourist pilgrimages; the export grammar shaped Korean stationery, Stationery TikTok, and every \"cute desk\" YouTube setup.",
